145:- `hyp add exp` under a Hypothesis is refused when the Experiment has no rubric: `--rubric REF` pins a bundle, `--rubric reading` says the body decides its verdicts by reading. `hyp add exp` is refused, at exit 1, when its `--recipe` is a bundle whose known answers expect a `verdict:` line (`a recipe measures and never prints a verdict; put the rule in a rubric`), when its `--rubric` names a bundle with a step section or not exactly one Standalone command, or a bundle with no known-answer case, or one where a label its cases print has no case printing `supports` or none printing `contradicts`, when `--rubric` names a bundle, the recipe is `none` and no `--records` is given (`the rubric has no measures to read: pin a recipe or --records`), and when `--rubric` or `--target` is given under a DesignQuestion (exit 2). `--records PATH` (repeatable) is read at add: a directory stands for each regular file under it, a path outside `.tree/evidence/` is refused (exit 2), and hyp stores each file's path and sha256; the new Experiment has no run yet, so every file it names is an earlier record. `--decided-by reading` is the older spelling of `--rubric reading` and writes `rubric: reading`. Replay does not check the rubric's presence: an Experiment without one in a merged log, or written before #106, still applies, and its Results record as before.
149:- Every write command is refused when its `--why`, `--body`, `--observation`, `--summary` or any `--limit` cites an id that is no node of the store. A cite is a token that starts at a word boundary with `G-`, `Q-`, `H-`, `E-` or `R-` and goes on with six upper-case Crockford base32 characters that end the word (a display id, §2), or with a placeholder mark: `<`, `{`, `…`, `...` or three or more `X`, as in `R-<new>`. A display id that resolves (§2) passes; a placeholder never does. Lower-case prose such as `E-mail` or `R-squared` is no cite, and a token right after `:` cites another store (`containment:R-ABC123`) and is not checked. The refusal names each flag and token, `--why names R-<new>, which is no node here`, appends nothing and exits 2, as `unknown node` does (§2). Text written before this rule, or by an older binary, replays unchanged.
151:`hyp add exp` also warns, without refusing, when its parent or a target is not a live rival (§7.1): it writes the Experiment, exits 0 and prints one `warn: D1:` line per such Hypothesis with its status, the Results that contradict it or the Hypotheses that supersede it. It reads the same rule as the D1 signal, so the warning and D1 name the same Hypotheses.
235:- **Constraints block.** A Goal body's constraints block runs from the first line that starts with `May change:`, `Read only:` or `Never:` to the end of the body. It is valid when it holds each of the three headings and every non-empty line in it starts with one of them. Replay stores no word for it and no status reads it. `hyp add goal` admits any body and warns when the body has a heading but no valid block. `hyp prompt G --actor A` prints the `/goal` prompt for G from G's node and the store's place in its repository: the store's path relative to the repository root, A, G, the skill hyp-goal-run, the stop clauses, and each block line as written, in body order; `--branch` and `--pr` add a clause each after the block. It refuses with exit 1 and nothing on stdout when the block is missing or not valid, naming each problem, and exits 2 with no `--actor` (it does not read `$HYP_ACTOR`), for an id that is not a Goal, or for a `--branch` that holds a space or a control character. It exits 3 when the store's directory cannot be resolved or no repository holds its real directory, so a store whose real directory is outside every repository exits 3 even when a link inside a repository reaches it. It checks the block first, so a Goal with no valid block exits 1 wherever the store is. Lines split on `\n`, and a line is empty only when it holds no byte, so a line of spaces, a tab, or the lone `\r` of a blank line in a CRLF body is a bad line inside the block. The store's path is read after resolving symbolic links, so a store reached through a linked directory prints the same bytes. Each block line prints as written, so a CRLF body with no blank line in its block keeps the `\r` at the end of each block line. It reads no answer, so the same Goal and flags print the same bytes before and after an answer and in a store with other Goals.
248:- **[added]** A matching hash says the procedure is the same, not that it gives the right answer. An **instrument** is a recipe bundle with an Evidence or Standalone command, the checks a verdict counts. A script whose output a Result's verdict counts lives in a bundle and runs as one of its commands (sandbox spec §1), never outside `recipes/`. An instrument must have known answers: cases under `known-answers/NAME/`, each an `input/` and an `expect.json` (`RECIPE-FORMAT.md`, Known answers). A frozen recipe whose cases print `verdict: LABEL=WORD` lines is a verdict check, and `hyp bundle check` warns when a label has no case printing `supports` or none printing `contradicts`, on a malformed verdict line and on a label printed twice in one case (ADR 0004); for a rubric it prints what `hyp add exp --rubric` refuses. Before every `hyp sandbox create` (for each stacked recipe, in stack order, before anything is made), `hyp bundle run` and `hyp bundle evidence`, hyp runs the instrument's known answers with the operator's hyp; a failed case refuses the run with exit 1, a timed-out case exits 3, and a refused create leaves no sandbox. `hyp add exp --recipe` refuses an instrument with no case. An Experiment under a Hypothesis declares how its verdicts are decided with its rubric, a bundle or `reading` (§5.1, next bullet). Replay never reads the declaration, and a false `--rubric reading` that hides a measure is not seen. The sandbox record and the command record both hold the outcome of each case, as `known_answers` (sandbox spec §5.1). A bundle without an Evidence or Standalone command is not gated (ADR 0002).
249:- **[added]** Recipe and rubric (ADR 0018). A recipe's checks print **measures**, never a verdict: one named line about one subject, such as `S1 fail` or `stored rows 4`. A **rubric** turns measures into verdicts: a bundle under `rubrics/` with no step section and one Standalone command, which reads the records it is given and prints one `verdict: LABEL=WORD` line per label. Each label its cases print needs a case printing `supports` and a case printing `contradicts`, which `hyp add exp --rubric` requires (§5.1), and its known answers gate every run, as a recipe's do. hyp never binds a label to a target and never reads a verdict line at run time: the runner types each target's verdict into `hyp record` (ADR 0004). An Experiment's rubric replaces `decided_by` and the older instrument test: an Experiment with targets declares a rubric, a bundle or `reading`, and one with no targets has none. A frozen recipe whose checks print verdicts stays history: Experiments that already pin it record as before, and no new Experiment pins it.

